 L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)
                    Simple definition

                    A child class should be able to replace its parent class without breaking the application.

                    This is probably the most confusing SOLID principle.

                    Let's use an easy example.

                    Suppose:

                    public class Bird {


                        public void fly() {
                            System.out.println("Flying");
                        }
                    }

                    Then:

                    public class Sparrow extends Bird {
                    }

                    Sparrow can fly, so everything is fine.

                    But now:

                    public class Penguin extends Bird {
                    }

                    Penguin cannot fly.

                    If we do:

                    Bird bird = new Penguin();


                    bird.fly();

                    Our design is broken.

                    The parent says:

                    Bird → can fly

                    But Penguin says:

                    Penguin → cannot fly

                    Therefore Penguin shouldn't inherit from a class that promises flying behavior.

                    ✅ Better design

                    Separate the concepts:

                    public class Bird {
                    }
                    public interface Flyable {
                        void fly();
                    }

                    Sparrow:

                    public class Sparrow extends Bird implements Flyable {


                        @Override
                        public void fly() {
                            System.out.println("Sparrow flying");
                        }
                    }

                    Penguin:

                    public class Penguin extends Bird {
                    }

                    Now:

                    Bird
                     ├── Sparrow → Flyable
                     │
                     └── Penguin

                    This follows LSP.

                    Easy way to remember

                    Ask:

                    "Can I replace the parent with the child without unexpected behavior?"

                    If yes → likely LSP.

                    If no → inheritance/design is probably wrong.